From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH, prototype 2] cocoa: do not hand the Shift that goes on to macOS to the input method

What: #657 hands every modifier event to mac-ime's input context inside the
tap and then passes Shift on to macOS. The input method therefore sees the
Shift from the app first and from the global monitor second. The order is
the other way round in the version that works (0xc00: the event is not in
the tap, so the global monitor sees it before the app hands it over).
This prototype keeps everything else and only skips the input context for
a Shift that the tap is about to pass on (`omacvm_ime_shift_would_pass()`
mirrors `omacvm_ime_shift_pass()` without its side effects). The guest's
modifier state still comes from `handleEventLocked`, unchanged.

Why: measured with WeChat Input and the independent probe (key 56, five
taps): hid-head, hid-tail, annot-listen, the NSEvent global monitor and
session-listen all see the Shift with flags 0x20102/0x100 and userData 0,
press to release 136 to 200 ms. mac-ime sees it as well, and the mode
changes 0 times. Dropping the copy (prototype 1) does not change that.

Upstream: not sent (a prototype for the discussion in #655 / #657).

Test: the same three checks as in #655.

--- a/ui/cocoa.m
